WEBVTT


00:00:11.036 --> 00:00:15.063
##


00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:39.058
(engine rumbling)


00:00:41.998 --> 00:00:44.830
MAN: When do you stop
looking at each other?


00:00:46.735 --> 00:00:49.670
Shouldn't there be a warning?


00:00:49.705 --> 00:00:51.070
Shouldn't somebody say to us,


00:00:51.106 --> 00:00:54.075
"Hey, watch out, pay attention"?


00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:57.043
Because you can be thinking,


00:00:57.078 --> 00:01:00.241
"I'm okay, we're okay,
we're good."


00:01:00.281 --> 00:01:04.411
Then you turn around and...
a distance between you.


00:01:19.466 --> 00:01:23.799
(indistinct conversation)


00:01:29.708 --> 00:01:33.940
##


00:01:40.351 --> 00:01:42.319
(boys shouting)


00:01:42.353 --> 00:01:43.615
(horn beeping)


00:01:43.655 --> 00:01:45.987
(shouting and cheering)


00:01:58.035 --> 00:02:00.435
(man speaking Bosnian)


00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:03.199
(speaking Bosnian)


00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:05.969
Zorany! Miro!


00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:11.937
I've got a little job for you.


00:02:24.359 --> 00:02:25.451
The Speed Merchants?


00:02:25.493 --> 00:02:27.551
I'm sorry, I apologize.
Traffic.


00:02:27.595 --> 00:02:30.359
Yeah, bad traffic
for three days?


00:02:30.397 --> 00:02:32.365
Hi, Bea.


00:02:32.399 --> 00:02:33.798
Hi, Dad.


00:02:33.834 --> 00:02:35.324
Very exciting.


00:02:42.342 --> 00:02:43.366
Okay, Bea.


00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:44.876
- No, I want...
- Stop with the balloon.


00:02:44.911 --> 00:02:46.037
No, stop, stop.


00:02:48.514 --> 00:02:50.539
MAN:
Who's that?


00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:51.880
- That's one of the cleaners.
- Yeah.


00:02:51.918 --> 00:02:52.941
It's the cleaner, yeah.


00:02:52.985 --> 00:02:54.009
Yeah, you know her--
Erika.


00:02:54.052 --> 00:02:55.041
She's staggering.


00:02:55.087 --> 00:02:56.554
Staggering.
Why do you have a picture


00:02:56.588 --> 00:02:58.215
of one of the cleaners
on your computer?


00:02:58.257 --> 00:02:59.656
I was just experimenting
with my camera.


00:02:59.691 --> 00:03:01.215
I was just, um, you know,


00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.522
"Can I just...?
Oh, that's good."


00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.296
Come on.


00:03:15.606 --> 00:03:16.573
It's all right for you, mate,


00:03:16.607 --> 00:03:17.574
you've got a gorgeous,
Swedish wife.


00:03:17.608 --> 00:03:18.574
Girlfriend.


00:03:18.608 --> 00:03:19.836
Half Swedish.


00:03:19.876 --> 00:03:22.242
You cannot say girlfriend
after ten years.


00:03:25.882 --> 00:03:27.713
You all right?


00:03:34.757 --> 00:03:35.883
I mean, isn't that true?


00:03:35.925 --> 00:03:37.688
You've been there,


00:03:37.793 --> 00:03:39.385
are you gassed in Sweden
if you're ugly?


00:03:39.428 --> 00:03:41.123
Or maybe just given a penis,


00:03:41.163 --> 00:03:43.859
because some of the men
can be very ugly,


00:03:43.899 --> 00:03:45.661
but I've never seen
an ugly Swedish woman.


00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.724
Jesus, look at Liv.


00:04:00.681 --> 00:04:05.812
(indistinct conversations)


00:04:18.698 --> 00:04:20.495
WOMAN:
Oh, I love this space.


00:04:20.533 --> 00:04:23.763
MAN: We do. We think it's
a steal. Thanks for coming.


00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.782
(banging)


00:04:48.825 --> 00:04:50.087
(glass breaking)


00:04:54.197 --> 00:04:56.165
(alarm beeping)


00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.131
(beeping continues)


00:05:11.079 --> 00:05:13.809
(beeping faster)


00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:19.411
(beeping continues)


00:05:20.554 --> 00:05:22.454
(tapping keys)


00:05:24.859 --> 00:05:26.486
(beeping stops)


00:05:26.527 --> 00:05:27.515
(knocking)


00:05:54.752 --> 00:05:56.117
That's us. Let's go!


00:06:29.952 --> 00:06:31.886
##


00:06:39.727 --> 00:06:42.127
(sighs)


00:06:50.571 --> 00:06:54.439
(fox yipping)


00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:02.404
Are we crazy?


00:07:02.448 --> 00:07:04.279
No.


00:07:04.317 --> 00:07:05.614
About what?


00:07:05.718 --> 00:07:07.345
King's Cross.


00:07:07.386 --> 00:07:09.251
Sandy still thinks we're
crazy to move there.


00:07:09.288 --> 00:07:11.448
It's so... the area.


00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.048
It's a great office, isn't it?


00:07:13.091 --> 00:07:14.353
And it was a...


00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:16.257
It's a great project,
it was a great night.


00:07:16.294 --> 00:07:17.261
I don't know.


00:07:17.295 --> 00:07:18.262
It's all great.


00:07:18.296 --> 00:07:19.422
(fox yipping)


00:07:19.464 --> 00:07:21.022
Thank you for making
an effort tonight.


00:07:21.066 --> 00:07:22.499
The launch,
I know it's not your...


00:07:22.534 --> 00:07:24.228
but it really helped.


00:07:24.268 --> 00:07:25.633
It's okay. It's fine.


00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:29.105
Was it an effort?


00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:30.300
What? That's her.


00:07:30.341 --> 00:07:31.308
(fox yipping)


00:07:31.342 --> 00:07:32.639
That's Bea.


00:07:32.743 --> 00:07:33.835
No, that's a fox.


00:07:33.878 --> 00:07:35.402
No, it's her,
she was exhausted.


00:07:35.446 --> 00:07:39.438
They're taking over London.
I hate that noise.


00:07:39.482 --> 00:07:41.109
(sighs):
It's so late.


00:07:41.151 --> 00:07:42.914
How can she just not sleep?


00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:48.289
I don't think going
to her always...


00:07:48.324 --> 00:07:50.348
I don't know if that helps.


00:07:50.392 --> 00:07:52.451
I know you don't.


00:07:52.494 --> 00:07:54.291
Let's go back and see someone.


00:07:54.329 --> 00:07:57.594
I've seen too many someones.


00:07:57.633 --> 00:07:59.157
Sorry.


00:07:59.201 --> 00:08:01.260
I'm sorry.


00:08:01.303 --> 00:08:05.238
Listen, what you're doing,
the whole project, for London,


00:08:05.273 --> 00:08:08.606
the office, everything--
it's wonderful.


00:08:08.709 --> 00:08:12.042
I think it can genuinely
be something...


00:08:12.079 --> 00:08:13.103
Rubbish.


00:08:13.147 --> 00:08:15.273
- Terrible.
- ...genuinely rubbish-like.


00:08:15.315 --> 00:08:16.282
(chuckles)


00:08:16.316 --> 00:08:17.374
Crap.


00:08:21.555 --> 00:08:22.852
I'll come back.


00:08:31.864 --> 00:08:35.391
Down, hold...


00:08:35.434 --> 00:08:38.267
one, two, three.


00:08:39.104 --> 00:08:43.472
Turn, one, two, three.


00:08:44.709 --> 00:08:47.769
Bea, darling, it's 3:30
in the morning.


00:08:47.812 --> 00:08:49.040
We have to stop this.


00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.047
One, two...


00:08:50.081 --> 00:08:53.016
(phone ringing)


00:08:53.917 --> 00:08:55.350
Hello?


00:08:55.385 --> 00:08:58.946
Yeah, well, no, I mean,
I'm his partner, so...


00:08:58.989 --> 00:09:00.547
Yes, I am.


00:09:00.591 --> 00:09:01.990
What?!


00:09:02.059 --> 00:09:03.924
Hi.


00:09:03.961 --> 00:09:05.326
Officer, this place
is my business.


00:09:05.362 --> 00:09:06.556
Go on, go ahead.


00:09:06.597 --> 00:09:07.927
- My partner's in there already.
- Okay.


00:09:07.964 --> 00:09:12.697
I had everything on that laptop,
my life is on that laptop.


00:09:12.735 --> 00:09:13.929
WOMAN:
I'm very sorry, sir.


00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:15.130
Will! Will!


00:09:15.171 --> 00:09:17.731
All the computers, my laptop,
the petty cash...


00:09:17.773 --> 00:09:18.967
Well, how did they get in?


00:09:19.008 --> 00:09:20.133
Through the roof.


00:09:20.175 --> 00:09:21.767
- What about the alarm?
- They turned it off.


00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.209
Well, how do they know
the code?


00:09:23.245 --> 00:09:24.439
I wasn't here.
I don't know.


00:09:24.479 --> 00:09:25.605
- Jesus Christ.
- Do you have cleaners?


00:09:25.714 --> 00:09:27.511
It's not the cleaners.


00:09:27.549 --> 00:09:30.017
This is covered in glass.


00:09:30.051 --> 00:09:31.951
It's not the cleaners.


00:09:31.987 --> 00:09:33.613
I know it's...
I know the cleaners.


00:09:33.654 --> 00:09:36.088
How can we have a break-in
on our first day?


00:09:36.123 --> 00:09:38.216
Unless the cleaners have turned
into a bunch of acrobats.


00:09:44.898 --> 00:09:45.864
MAN:
Who is it?


00:09:45.898 --> 00:09:46.956
It's Zoran and Miro.


00:09:52.605 --> 00:09:56.632
(speaking Bosnian)


00:10:00.879 --> 00:10:04.110
- Hey.
- Hey.


00:10:06.952 --> 00:10:09.648
How's my best nephew, hmm?


00:10:09.721 --> 00:10:11.086
- Dragan.
- Huh?


00:10:11.123 --> 00:10:15.559
See my hands? See his?


00:10:15.593 --> 00:10:16.560
Uh-huh.


00:10:16.594 --> 00:10:19.495
His dad had the big brains.


00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.090
I had the big hands.


00:10:22.133 --> 00:10:27.195
Your dad had hands
like a girl's.


00:10:27.237 --> 00:10:30.434
And the girls loved him.


00:10:30.473 --> 00:10:31.906
It's the same with you two.


00:10:31.941 --> 00:10:35.399
My son is the clumsy one.


00:10:35.445 --> 00:10:37.241
(tapes clatter on floor)


00:10:41.483 --> 00:10:42.472
This is for you.


00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.288
Wipe everything.


00:10:46.322 --> 00:10:47.687
You know how to do that?


00:10:47.723 --> 00:10:48.815
Erase everything?


00:10:48.857 --> 00:10:51.256
They'll be delivering
new computers,


00:10:51.292 --> 00:10:54.159
and then you can go back in,
my little monkey.


00:10:59.667 --> 00:11:02.659
(TV playing)


00:11:02.704 --> 00:11:03.761
Hi, Mom.


00:11:17.016 --> 00:11:18.449
How was school?


00:11:18.484 --> 00:11:20.281
It was okay.


00:11:20.319 --> 00:11:21.581
Lots of homework?


00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:23.554
No, not really.


00:11:23.589 --> 00:11:25.580
Not really?


00:11:27.593 --> 00:11:29.492
Mirsad,


00:11:29.527 --> 00:11:32.189
you've not been
at school for weeks.


00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:33.959
They sent another letter.


00:11:33.998 --> 00:11:35.465
It's a mistake-- half the time


00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.331
they don't know who's there
and who's not there.


00:11:42.506 --> 00:11:45.669
Now we have
to go back to see people


00:11:45.709 --> 00:11:47.199
at the Social Services.


00:11:48.345 --> 00:11:51.212
We survived.


00:11:51.248 --> 00:11:54.513
Do you understand?


00:11:54.551 --> 00:11:56.177
We survived,


00:11:56.219 --> 00:11:58.312
and that's not free.


00:11:58.354 --> 00:12:00.151
I didn't ask to survive.


00:12:00.189 --> 00:12:02.885
Imagine your father.


00:12:02.925 --> 00:12:05.359
If we could offer him
just one day,


00:12:05.395 --> 00:12:07.488
one hour-- imagine...


00:12:08.063 --> 00:12:10.554
You have a life and you are
throwing it away.


00:12:10.599 --> 00:12:12.430
I don't even remember him.


00:12:12.467 --> 00:12:15.129
I don't remember him.


00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.933
Where do you go?


00:12:16.972 --> 00:12:18.564
Where do you go every day?


00:12:18.607 --> 00:12:19.869
I'm working, I'm earning money.


00:12:19.908 --> 00:12:21.374
You have to go to school.


00:12:21.409 --> 00:12:22.876
That's what we promised,


00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:24.207
with police and the court.


00:12:24.245 --> 00:12:25.872
Otherwise, they'll
put you in prison.


00:12:25.913 --> 00:12:27.540
I'll be 16 in three months.


00:12:27.581 --> 00:12:30.550
Mirsad...


00:12:30.584 --> 00:12:31.744
You're a clever boy.


00:12:31.786 --> 00:12:33.753
You're clever,
you've got a brain.


00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:36.015
I have to go. I'm late.


00:12:36.055 --> 00:12:38.956
If somebody,
if one of his family,


00:12:38.992 --> 00:12:41.017
if any of our people are
leading you, I'm telling you,


00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.358
if anyone of them is,
I'll kill them!


00:12:43.396 --> 00:12:45.762
And I wouldn't care!


00:12:55.841 --> 00:12:59.367
(man speaking Bosnian)


00:13:20.363 --> 00:13:26.130
(indistinct audio from computer)


00:13:26.168 --> 00:13:30.434
LIV: We've never really
had a proper diagnosis,


00:13:30.473 --> 00:13:32.873
but I've always known
Bea was special.


00:13:32.908 --> 00:13:34.569
Yeah!


00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:37.579
She's double happy, double sad,
double excited, double awake,


00:13:37.613 --> 00:13:43.209
double needy-- well, no,
triple needy, actually.


00:13:43.251 --> 00:13:44.946
(laughs)


00:13:44.986 --> 00:13:46.283
I know even...


00:13:46.321 --> 00:13:47.845
even when Bea
was ten months old,


00:13:47.889 --> 00:13:51.517
she, she was... already
distressed by noise.


00:13:51.558 --> 00:13:55.187
Sh-She would only sleep with
a particular blanket next to her


00:13:55.229 --> 00:13:58.323
or-or eat
with a particular spoon.


00:13:58.365 --> 00:14:01.801
Lately it's been getting worse
and she can't sleep at all.


00:14:01.835 --> 00:14:04.598
She can't manage at school,
where, uh...


00:14:04.704 --> 00:14:09.141
all of us, just completely
at our wits' end.


00:14:09.175 --> 00:14:12.611
I can't, I'm sorry, I'm late.


00:14:12.645 --> 00:14:17.081
(indistinct conversation)


00:14:17.115 --> 00:14:19.447
(heavy machinery running)


00:14:24.756 --> 00:14:26.621
...eat foods of a certain color.


00:14:26.658 --> 00:14:28.558
Sorry.


00:14:29.561 --> 00:14:31.221
Sorry.


00:14:35.232 --> 00:14:37.860
I'm Rosemary McCloud.


00:14:37.902 --> 00:14:39.267
Hi, Rosemary.
I'm Will.


00:14:39.303 --> 00:14:40.565
Where's Bea?


00:14:40.604 --> 00:14:42.037
Oh, no, Bea...


00:14:42.072 --> 00:14:44.198
No, the structure is
that before I begin


00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.798
any sort of diagnosis,


00:14:45.842 --> 00:14:48.037
I'd like to meet
the parents first, alone.


00:14:48.077 --> 00:14:49.476
Right, great.


00:14:49.512 --> 00:14:53.039
So I'm just, uh,
finishing with Liv.


00:14:53.082 --> 00:14:54.413
Um...


00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:57.646
Well, I'd like to, uh, listen
to these documentaries.


00:14:57.719 --> 00:14:59.812
I must have heard them
in the past.


00:14:59.855 --> 00:15:01.618
Uh, no, they're mostly
World Service.


00:15:01.657 --> 00:15:04.683
Liv explained that she's going
to semi-retire until Bea gets...


00:15:04.726 --> 00:15:05.715
That's not true.


00:15:05.761 --> 00:15:07.820
- Effectively.
- Will...


00:15:07.863 --> 00:15:10.194
It's, it's either
Bea's gymnastics,


00:15:10.231 --> 00:15:11.198
Bea's art therapy,


00:15:11.232 --> 00:15:12.529
Bea's music therapy,


00:15:12.567 --> 00:15:14.125
it's a sacrifice
is all I'm saying.


00:15:14.168 --> 00:15:17.035
Liv won prizes for the work
she did, did she tell you that?


00:15:17.071 --> 00:15:19.505
It's a big sacrifice
is all I'm saying, hmm.


00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.007
I was just explaining
to Rosemary...


00:15:21.042 --> 00:15:22.565
She's up all night.


00:15:22.609 --> 00:15:26.375
...that, um, in these past few
months, that Bea's gotten worse.


00:15:26.413 --> 00:15:28.278
- Hmm.
- Yeah.


00:15:28.315 --> 00:15:34.116
And, uh, she's not sleeping,
and... it's intense.


00:15:34.153 --> 00:15:35.848
Yes.


00:15:35.888 --> 00:15:37.378
Hav-Have either
of you-- therapy--


00:15:37.423 --> 00:15:38.822
have either of you considered


00:15:38.857 --> 00:15:40.950
ever-ever thought
about doing that or...?


00:15:40.993 --> 00:15:42.324
I did see someone.


00:15:42.361 --> 00:15:43.589
When?


00:15:43.629 --> 00:15:47.086
For a while,
'cause I can get low sometimes.


00:15:47.131 --> 00:15:49.065
- I just go into low...
- Wow.


00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:50.897
Um, my father died
and my mother died


00:15:50.935 --> 00:15:52.402
- and my sister died and...
- Hmm.


00:15:52.437 --> 00:15:55.634
It's family with
a short life expectancy.


00:15:55.673 --> 00:15:58.141
And a grandmother of 93.


00:15:58.176 --> 00:16:00.609
And a grandmother
of 93.


00:16:00.644 --> 00:16:06.549
But some days the cup is empty
and some days its 93% full.


00:16:10.253 --> 00:16:12.585
Were you, um, were you
brought up in Sweden or...?


00:16:12.623 --> 00:16:17.184
Oh, my father was American,
and I went to university there.


00:16:17.226 --> 00:16:18.284
- Uh-huh.
- Chicago.


00:16:18.327 --> 00:16:20.818
Addicted to cold.


00:16:20.863 --> 00:16:23.229
No.


00:16:25.167 --> 00:16:28.795
Uh, are you concerned about Bea?


00:16:28.837 --> 00:16:31.533
Sure, you know, but Liv is...


00:16:31.573 --> 00:16:33.097
(sighs)


00:16:33.141 --> 00:16:37.475
Liv worries so much, there's,
there's not much room... for...


00:16:37.512 --> 00:16:39.479
You know,
but a 13-year-old girl


00:16:39.513 --> 00:16:41.378
who collects batteries,


00:16:41.415 --> 00:16:47.479
doesn't sleep, doesn't let us
sleep, won't eat anything,


00:16:47.521 --> 00:16:49.785
spends all day wanting
to do somersaults, I mean,


00:16:49.823 --> 00:16:51.256
yeah, sure, I'm concerned.


00:16:51.291 --> 00:16:52.587
Of course I'm concerned.


00:16:52.625 --> 00:16:57.392
Liv says that you're wrapped up
in your work.


00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:00.627
Wrapped up?
Wrapped up?


00:17:00.667 --> 00:17:02.464
That's fair.


00:17:02.502 --> 00:17:04.163
I love how working hard,
working really hard,


00:17:04.203 --> 00:17:06.295
becomes wrapped up,
becomes something selfish.


00:17:06.338 --> 00:17:07.305
I love that.


00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.933
When you say that you love it,


00:17:09.975 --> 00:17:12.273
do you mean
that you don't love it?


00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:16.677
Let's just back up.


00:17:16.715 --> 00:17:18.238
We love Bea.


00:17:18.282 --> 00:17:21.251
It's not a competition to see
who loves her the most.


00:17:21.285 --> 00:17:25.244
If it were a competition,
I would happily let Liv win.


00:17:25.289 --> 00:17:27.280
You win.


00:17:31.394 --> 00:17:34.022
MAN: It says here your father
died, your husband,


00:17:34.064 --> 00:17:36.589
your sister.


00:17:36.666 --> 00:17:38.793
This was where? Sarajevo?


00:17:38.835 --> 00:17:42.362
Um, Miro and I left
with the Red Cross.


00:17:42.405 --> 00:17:46.500
My husband stayed
behind and was, uh,


00:17:46.542 --> 00:17:49.477
he stayed behind
and was murdered.


00:17:49.511 --> 00:17:51.138
You're Serbs?


00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:54.513
My husband was Serb.
I'm Muslim.


00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:56.244
Uh, it's complicated.


00:17:56.284 --> 00:17:58.946
We're Bosnian,
we're all Bosnian...


00:17:58.986 --> 00:18:00.453
It's complicated.


00:18:00.488 --> 00:18:02.547
Do you have
relatives in Britain?


00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:06.117
My husband's family.


00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:10.357
His brother, sisters and aunt.


00:18:10.397 --> 00:18:12.558
Was a time when
a smack around the ear


00:18:12.666 --> 00:18:15.362
or my auntie would
give me a thump.


00:18:15.402 --> 00:18:18.269
Matter of fact, she punctured
my eardrum at one point,


00:18:18.305 --> 00:18:19.567
but you knew where you were.


00:18:19.639 --> 00:18:20.901
Simple, we're all simple.


00:18:20.941 --> 00:18:23.568
What about you, Sonny Jim?


00:18:23.642 --> 00:18:26.076
What have you been up to while
you've not been at school?


00:18:26.112 --> 00:18:30.947
Back thieving,
stealing again? Hmm?


00:18:30.983 --> 00:18:33.474
See, things are not things.


00:18:33.519 --> 00:18:37.113
Things are always
somebody's things.


00:18:37.155 --> 00:18:40.147
Car is Jack's car,
'cause he worked for it.


00:18:40.191 --> 00:18:42.819
Your Prada jacket is little
Kevin's Prada jacket,


00:18:42.861 --> 00:18:46.353
before it was his-- I can see
your little brain ticking over.


00:18:46.397 --> 00:18:49.422
His Prada jacket
belonged to Mr. Prada.


00:18:49.466 --> 00:18:52.060
What about before that?


00:18:52.102 --> 00:18:53.592
Before what?


00:18:53.704 --> 00:18:57.105
Before it was Mr. Prada's?


00:18:57.141 --> 00:18:59.939
Did it belong to the women
who made it?


00:18:59.977 --> 00:19:04.106
'Cause my mom does that--
makes clothes for other people,


00:19:04.147 --> 00:19:05.842
slaves for other people.


00:19:05.882 --> 00:19:07.713
Or like when prisoners
make trains.


00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:10.048
Next time you're going down.


00:19:10.086 --> 00:19:11.383
We'll catch you,


00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.445
and then you can discuss
who owns the trains


00:19:13.489 --> 00:19:14.455
with your cell mates.


00:19:14.489 --> 00:19:15.456
(water bottle smacks)


00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:17.151
Back in a minute.


00:19:24.533 --> 00:19:26.727
Sorry I'm late again,
but everything is here.


00:19:26.767 --> 00:19:27.995
- All the Macs. Everything
- It's okay.


00:19:28.035 --> 00:19:30.026
- Excellent. Thank you.
- Thank you.


00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:31.503
Excuse me.
You dropped this.


00:19:31.539 --> 00:19:34.565
Oh, thanks, Erika.


00:19:40.113 --> 00:19:43.139
Oh, please, come up, Erika.
It is Erika, isn't it?


00:19:43.183 --> 00:19:45.014
- Yes, sir.
- Sandy.


00:19:51.691 --> 00:19:53.487
We set the alarm, sir.


00:19:53.525 --> 00:19:55.083
I did it myself.


00:19:55.127 --> 00:19:56.253
You have my word.


00:19:56.295 --> 00:19:57.592
I'm not accusing you.


00:19:57.629 --> 00:19:59.790
The police have already
called my mobile phone,


00:19:59.832 --> 00:20:01.094
asking me questions.


00:20:01.133 --> 00:20:02.100
Yeah, that is normal.


00:20:02.134 --> 00:20:03.601
About this thing
and then that thing.


00:20:03.635 --> 00:20:04.602
I'm so sorry.


00:20:04.636 --> 00:20:05.602
It was like Kafka.


00:20:05.636 --> 00:20:06.864
"Where did you get your car?"


00:20:06.904 --> 00:20:08.872
"Why are you ordering takeaway?"


00:20:08.906 --> 00:20:10.806
You know, we try
to do a good job.


00:20:10.842 --> 00:20:13.606
You-you do, you do do
a very good job.


00:20:13.678 --> 00:20:15.612
Erika, listen.


00:20:15.646 --> 00:20:18.512
We have to have a new code
for the alarm.


00:20:18.548 --> 00:20:20.413
Now, we're going to give you
your own code,


00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:21.883
You're going to choose it,
not us,


00:20:21.918 --> 00:20:23.283
so there's absolutely no way


00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:24.981
of you being wrongly accused
of anything.


00:20:25.021 --> 00:20:26.852
- Is that a deal?
- Okay, thanks.


00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:28.050
Oh, listen.


00:20:28.091 --> 00:20:32.527
I had your picture
on my computer.


00:20:32.561 --> 00:20:34.222
What would the burglar
make of that?


00:20:34.263 --> 00:20:35.628
My photograph?


00:20:35.664 --> 00:20:37.928
Yeah. Well, from when I was,
um, trying my camera out,


00:20:37.967 --> 00:20:39.298
if you remember.


00:20:39.335 --> 00:20:41.997
So, the burglar, someone
who didn't know the context,


00:20:42.037 --> 00:20:45.335
would think you were
a friend or a girlfriend.


00:20:45.373 --> 00:20:47.398
I mean, someone standing over
there who couldn't hear us,


00:20:47.442 --> 00:20:49.637
what would he make of this?


00:20:49.677 --> 00:20:51.008
Well, apart from my uniform.


00:20:51.045 --> 00:20:52.512
Of course apart
from the uniform, yeah.


00:20:52.547 --> 00:20:55.072
But say, if we were too far away
to see the uniform.


00:20:57.718 --> 00:20:59.743
Y-You know? And I could give you
this book, and you know,


00:20:59.786 --> 00:21:01.651
for all matey-boy over there
knows, that's a collected works


00:21:01.722 --> 00:21:02.814
of Franz Kafka,
that's your birthday...


00:21:02.856 --> 00:21:05.620
- Or the Bible.
- Or the Bible.


00:21:09.696 --> 00:21:11.026
Uh...


00:21:31.549 --> 00:21:33.346
WILL:
The model hasn't changed.


00:21:33.385 --> 00:21:34.352
Of course I know.


00:21:34.386 --> 00:21:35.444
Because we'd have to plant...


00:21:35.487 --> 00:21:36.510
Why isn't she moving?


00:21:36.553 --> 00:21:38.521
You can't take a fish
out of the water.


00:21:38.555 --> 00:21:39.783
I have 50 e-mails.


00:21:39.823 --> 00:21:41.120
- Why isn't she moving?
- She's dead.


00:21:41.158 --> 00:21:42.989
You can't take
a fish out of the water.


00:21:43.027 --> 00:21:44.619
- Okay, put her down.
- Darling, listen to Mommy.


00:21:44.662 --> 00:21:46.857
No, no, you can't take
a fish out of the water.


00:21:46.897 --> 00:21:48.262
No, no, don't
put her back in.


00:21:48.299 --> 00:21:49.856
You can't take
a fish out of water!


00:21:49.899 --> 00:21:51.025
She needs water.


00:21:51.067 --> 00:21:53.035
Sandy, no, I can't
get to the office.


00:21:53.069 --> 00:21:54.900
If you can get
to the office for me.


00:21:54.938 --> 00:21:56.337
Well, I can't because
I'm needed here.


00:21:56.372 --> 00:21:57.964
You're right, you
can't take a fish out.


00:21:58.007 --> 00:21:59.634
You can't, but she's dead.
See? She's dead.


00:21:59.676 --> 00:22:01.734
You can go? Okay,
that's great. Thank you.


00:22:01.777 --> 00:22:02.903
- It's okay. We'll bury her.
- I'll speak later.


00:22:02.945 --> 00:22:04.537
Okay, this is what
we're going to do.


00:22:04.579 --> 00:22:06.410
We're going to put her
in a little box.


00:22:06.448 --> 00:22:08.575
Oh, don't cry, darling.


00:22:08.617 --> 00:22:09.948
Don't cry.


00:22:09.985 --> 00:22:12.010
Come on, we're going
to put her in a box...


00:22:12.054 --> 00:22:13.112
(voice trails off)


00:22:15.923 --> 00:22:19.086
(shuffling)


00:22:19.126 --> 00:22:20.684
(sniffing)


00:22:20.728 --> 00:22:22.491
(indistinct conversation)


00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:25.897
New code.
Two, one...


00:22:25.933 --> 00:22:28.492
seven, eight.


00:22:28.535 --> 00:22:30.469
It's my date of birth.


00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:35.167
(door slamming)


00:22:35.208 --> 00:22:36.197
(beeping stops)


00:22:36.242 --> 00:22:40.234
(distant chatter)


00:22:49.989 --> 00:22:53.981
(alarm blaring)


00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:03.992
(alarm stops)


00:23:12.743 --> 00:23:14.210
Now what are you doing?


00:23:14.244 --> 00:23:15.370
(rapid honking)


00:23:15.412 --> 00:23:16.709
Let's go! Leave it.


00:23:19.749 --> 00:23:20.943
Come on, let's go!


00:23:20.983 --> 00:23:22.109
(engine starting)


00:23:22.151 --> 00:23:23.948
Come on!


00:23:23.986 --> 00:23:24.680
Get the door!


00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:28.789
Oh!


00:23:28.824 --> 00:23:31.224
Jesus Christ!


00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.386
Fucking hell.


00:23:33.428 --> 00:23:37.194
(dog barking)


00:23:37.232 --> 00:23:38.290
Oh...


00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:54.311
MAN:
This is brilliant.


00:23:54.348 --> 00:23:56.111
You're really going
to do all this, I mean,


00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:57.515
put a canal right
through the middle?


00:23:57.551 --> 00:23:59.711
Yeah.


00:23:59.752 --> 00:24:02.084
It's brilliant.


00:24:02.121 --> 00:24:04.385
I'm Bruno Fella, CID.


00:24:04.424 --> 00:24:05.550
Are you Sandy or Will?


00:24:05.625 --> 00:24:06.557
- I'm Will.
- Hello, Will.


00:24:06.626 --> 00:24:08.560
- Hi.
- You know what?


00:24:08.594 --> 00:24:12.085
I was born right there,
just about there.


00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:13.358
I'd have drowned.


00:24:13.398 --> 00:24:14.763
Mm.


00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:15.767
I love it.


00:24:15.801 --> 00:24:17.029
I think it's really nice.


00:24:17.069 --> 00:24:20.368
This is Sandy, um,
Hoffman, my partner.


00:24:20.405 --> 00:24:21.565
- Sandy.
- Hello.


00:24:21.606 --> 00:24:22.573
Detective Bruno Fella, CID.


00:24:22.607 --> 00:24:23.766
Hi, Bruno.


00:24:23.808 --> 00:24:26.140
I'm just saying this
is a fabulous building.


00:24:26.177 --> 00:24:27.201
I think it's brilliant.


00:24:27.244 --> 00:24:28.404
Thank you.


00:24:28.446 --> 00:24:30.107
Was it you had
the scrape with the villains?


00:24:30.147 --> 00:24:31.739
No, I wouldn't say
it was a scrape.


00:24:31.782 --> 00:24:32.908
Well, they scraped my car.


00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:34.815
No, I was running late
for the first break-in.


00:24:34.852 --> 00:24:36.251
I wasn't quite
prepared for the second.


00:24:36.287 --> 00:24:37.344
Well, I get it, completely.


00:24:37.387 --> 00:24:39.514
You know,
completely get it, you know?


00:24:39.556 --> 00:24:40.750
You're, um, disgruntled.


00:24:40.790 --> 00:24:43.054
I would be, you know,
putting all that effort


00:24:43.093 --> 00:24:44.685
into making such a fabulous
building, you know,


00:24:44.727 --> 00:24:46.354
in an off-style community.


00:24:46.396 --> 00:24:48.261
You know, you don't
need the grief, do you?


00:24:48.298 --> 00:24:49.594
Well, it's no fun, Bruno.


00:24:49.698 --> 00:24:50.892
You know, financially
it's a disaster.


00:24:50.933 --> 00:24:52.525
The insurance is gonna be
through the roof.


00:24:52.568 --> 00:24:53.796
You know your problem, Sandy?


00:24:53.836 --> 00:24:55.531
What's my problem?


00:24:55.571 --> 00:24:56.560
King's Cross.


00:24:56.605 --> 00:24:57.867
Is what?
What is it?


00:24:57.906 --> 00:24:59.931
I mean... that's you there.


00:24:59.975 --> 00:25:01.806
You got a British
Library over there


00:25:01.844 --> 00:25:03.640
with Eurostar,
and bang in the middle


00:25:03.678 --> 00:25:05.737
you got crack village
with a load of Somalians


00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:06.906
walking about with machetes.


00:25:06.948 --> 00:25:08.381
It's an area in flux.


00:25:08.416 --> 00:25:11.214
WILL: Oh, they left
a computer disk, the burglars.


00:25:11.252 --> 00:25:12.879
Did you know that?
With my photographs.


00:25:12.920 --> 00:25:14.387
Oh. What do you mean? Where?


00:25:14.422 --> 00:25:16.218
Sorry, they <i>returned</i> it.


00:25:16.256 --> 00:25:18.451
More than that, they downloaded
my personal photographs


00:25:18.492 --> 00:25:20.323
off my laptop, that they
stole the first time,


00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:21.349
and they brought it back.


00:25:21.394 --> 00:25:22.383
Well, can we see it?


00:25:22.429 --> 00:25:23.487
Yeah.


00:25:24.598 --> 00:25:26.259
Lovely.


00:25:29.268 --> 00:25:31.327
So they got compassion.


00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:32.997
I won't go that far.


00:25:33.038 --> 00:25:35.268
Well, I'm not saying you gotta
show them compassion.


00:25:35.307 --> 00:25:38.105
You know, you're just like
my girlfriend, my ex-girlfriend.


00:25:38.144 --> 00:25:41.579
<i>They're</i> the criminals,
<i>they're</i> the bad guys.


00:25:41.613 --> 00:25:42.875
Can I have a go?


00:25:44.616 --> 00:25:46.675
There...
Don't you just love a Mac?


00:25:46.718 --> 00:25:47.912
Sweet, huh?


00:25:47.952 --> 00:25:49.817
And what do you say
to your girlfriend?


00:25:49.854 --> 00:25:51.253
I'm the Old Bill,
Will, you know?


00:25:51.289 --> 00:25:53.587
I've been on the beat,
undercover, in a suit,


00:25:53.625 --> 00:25:54.921
you know, it's the law.


00:25:54.958 --> 00:25:57.756
It's, uh, Einstein's
law of relativity.


00:25:57.794 --> 00:26:00.592
What does that mean,
exactly, sir? Relativity?


00:26:00.631 --> 00:26:03.293
Well, relatively speaking, um,
it means, if me and Sandy here


00:26:03.333 --> 00:26:06.461
break the law, you know, we're
gonna get a decent lawyer.


00:26:06.503 --> 00:26:07.469
Hmm.


00:26:07.503 --> 00:26:08.902
Now these guys
who broke in here,


00:26:08.938 --> 00:26:10.997
they go straight to jail.
"Do not pass Go."


00:26:14.243 --> 00:26:16.803
One law for us, one for them.


00:26:16.846 --> 00:26:19.041
Except we haven't
broken the law.


00:26:19.081 --> 00:26:20.570
Everyone's broken the law.


00:26:22.150 --> 00:26:23.549
WILL (on computer):
Our vision for King's Cross,


00:26:23.585 --> 00:26:24.643
for the public spaces


00:26:24.686 --> 00:26:26.881
of King's Cross,
starts with the premise


00:26:26.922 --> 00:26:31.791
that we acknowledge an urban
landscape is a <i>built</i> landscape.


00:26:31.826 --> 00:26:34.885
It starts as an argument
with society's


00:26:34.929 --> 00:26:36.590
phony love affair with nature.


00:26:39.266 --> 00:26:42.565
We are against the mistaking
of grass for nature,


00:26:42.603 --> 00:26:44.571
of green for nature.


00:26:44.605 --> 00:26:47.437
King's Cross is
an area of North London


00:26:47.473 --> 00:26:52.342
associated with poverty,
crime, vice and urban decay.


00:26:54.681 --> 00:26:58.242
Our job is to
transform the landscape,


00:26:58.284 --> 00:27:01.343
not decorate it with green...


00:27:01.386 --> 00:27:04.753
because how we feel about
ourselves, how we behave,


00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:08.055
is directly affected
by the space around us.


00:27:08.093 --> 00:27:09.754
How we design
the outdoors of our city


00:27:09.795 --> 00:27:14.254
is as important
as how we design the indoors.


00:27:14.298 --> 00:27:16.858
We're gonna take the canal,
and use it like calligraphy,


00:27:16.901 --> 00:27:20.564
like ink, to write
around the development.


00:27:26.977 --> 00:27:28.171
MAN:
We think it's the cleaners.


00:27:28.211 --> 00:27:29.200
WILL:
What about the cleaners?


00:27:29.246 --> 00:27:30.213
Breaking in.


00:27:30.247 --> 00:27:32.215
They don't even clean properly.


00:27:32.249 --> 00:27:33.216
It's true.
They don't.


00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:34.547
They never empty
the dishwasher--


00:27:34.584 --> 00:27:35.778
or load it,
for that matter.


00:27:35.819 --> 00:27:37.547
They bring their kids,
their boyfriends.


00:27:37.586 --> 00:27:39.110
- Come on.
- They do.


00:27:39.154 --> 00:27:40.553
And to be honest, Will,


00:27:40.589 --> 00:27:41.886
it's getting
a little traumatic.


00:27:41.924 --> 00:27:43.949
I'm staying late
at the office and worrying


00:27:43.993 --> 00:27:46.188
that the place is gonna be
ransacked while I'm in it.


00:27:46.228 --> 00:27:48.128
Look, they're cleaners
and they're not clean.


00:27:48.163 --> 00:27:49.425
WILL:
Okay.


00:27:49.465 --> 00:27:50.897
Good, thanks.


00:27:50.932 --> 00:27:52.900
Thanks for the tea,
thanks for the theory.


00:27:52.934 --> 00:27:54.561
I'm sorry, but what
does that mean?


00:27:54.602 --> 00:27:57.230
It means <i>your</i> boyfriend's
been to the office, Ruby,


00:27:57.272 --> 00:27:58.569
and yours, Wei Ping,


00:27:58.606 --> 00:27:59.903
and yours, actually, Joe,


00:27:59.941 --> 00:28:02.239
so where do the rules say
we <i>can</i> have boyfriends,


00:28:02.277 --> 00:28:04.506
but the cleaners can't?


00:28:04.544 --> 00:28:05.511
Or kids.


00:28:05.545 --> 00:28:08.309
And... explain this
to me, please.


00:28:08.348 --> 00:28:10.816
How the cleaners are
getting up onto the roof


00:28:10.851 --> 00:28:13.911
then swinging through the
roof line, and <i>why</i> they are,


00:28:13.954 --> 00:28:16.945
when they've already
got the keys and codes?


00:28:19.091 --> 00:28:20.058
All right.


00:28:20.092 --> 00:28:21.525
Hi.


00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:23.528
I've just been trying
to defend our cleaners.


00:28:23.596 --> 00:28:24.858
Uh-huh.


00:28:24.897 --> 00:28:28.389
Well, I've just spoken
to the alarm company.


00:28:28.434 --> 00:28:31.766
Whoever broke in used
the cleaners' code.


00:28:35.006 --> 00:28:37.406
WILL: What do we actually do
if we see a burglar?


00:28:37.442 --> 00:28:40.206
Call the police?
Kill them?


00:28:40.245 --> 00:28:41.734
SANDY:
There's a weird ecology,


00:28:41.779 --> 00:28:44.543
of course, to these break-ins.


00:28:44.581 --> 00:28:46.549
The new computers,
they're upgrades,


00:28:46.583 --> 00:28:47.880
you know, they're new models.


00:28:47.918 --> 00:28:49.886
You could argue that...


00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:51.888
a break-in every,
oh, say, six months,


00:28:51.922 --> 00:28:53.219
it's good business, isn't it?


00:28:53.257 --> 00:28:54.917
Just not every six days.


00:28:54.957 --> 00:28:58.893
I wish they would steal
this car, for instance.


00:28:58.928 --> 00:29:00.691
42,000 miles.


00:29:04.867 --> 00:29:07.494
I've got the criminal mind.


00:29:07.536 --> 00:29:11.370
Lots of wanting to be bad.


00:29:11.406 --> 00:29:13.169
See an ass, want to bite it.


00:29:13.208 --> 00:29:14.197
(chuckles)


00:29:15.877 --> 00:29:18.345
I just never do.


00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:19.369
Good.


00:29:20.548 --> 00:29:22.709
Good.


00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:24.183
What you've done,
there is you strayed


00:29:24.218 --> 00:29:26.379
from the moral to the criminal,


00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:27.648
and you crossed
the moral-criminal divide.


00:29:27.688 --> 00:29:29.519
Wanting to bite an ass
is a <i>moral</i> issue,


00:29:29.557 --> 00:29:31.388
and then only if the ass
belongs to someone


00:29:31.425 --> 00:29:32.517
who objects to the idea.


00:29:32.626 --> 00:29:35.526
Criminal would be biting
the ass without permission.


00:29:35.562 --> 00:29:36.529
You're such a lawyer.


00:29:36.563 --> 00:29:38.190
Yeah.


00:29:38.231 --> 00:29:40.028
Anyway, then what?


00:29:40.066 --> 00:29:41.033
Then what, what?


00:29:41.067 --> 00:29:44.730
You bite the ass, then what?


00:29:44.771 --> 00:29:47.830
Well, then... they bite yours.


00:29:47.873 --> 00:29:49.738
Is the theory.


00:29:49.775 --> 00:29:55.543
It's so long since
I've bitten or been bit...


00:29:55.614 --> 00:29:57.912
It's good to talk about that.


00:29:57.950 --> 00:29:59.177
Mm.


00:29:59.217 --> 00:30:02.516
The problem is, you find out
we're all so miserable.


00:30:08.192 --> 00:30:09.921
Hello, hello.


00:30:09.961 --> 00:30:11.758
He doesn't look happy.


00:30:11.796 --> 00:30:13.057
Do you recognize him?


00:30:13.096 --> 00:30:15.121
Uh... no.
I don't know.


00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:18.090
God, I wish I could lip-read.


00:30:19.269 --> 00:30:22.238
Hang on.


00:30:22.272 --> 00:30:23.398
I think I <i>can</i>.


00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:26.772
"I don't like you anymore.


00:30:26.809 --> 00:30:28.242
"I like Sandy.


00:30:28.277 --> 00:30:31.576
"He's everything
I want in a man.


00:30:31.614 --> 00:30:33.241
"And stop burgling
his bloody office."


00:30:33.282 --> 00:30:34.510
(knocking)


00:30:34.550 --> 00:30:35.915
God.


00:30:35.951 --> 00:30:37.884
(both laughing)


00:30:40.655 --> 00:30:41.587
Hello.


00:30:41.656 --> 00:30:43.590
Got a light?


00:30:43.625 --> 00:30:44.956
We don't smoke. Sorry.


00:30:44.993 --> 00:30:46.255
But there is the car lighter.


00:30:49.964 --> 00:30:51.021
All right.


00:30:51.064 --> 00:30:52.361
So is it?


00:30:52.399 --> 00:30:53.593
'Course it is.


00:30:53.634 --> 00:30:54.601
I don't believe it.


00:30:54.668 --> 00:30:55.828
Okay.


00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:02.609
I've got nothing on
under my coat.


00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:06.879
Well, this,
this might warm you up.


00:31:06.913 --> 00:31:08.210
Christ.


00:31:08.247 --> 00:31:10.078
Oh, God.


00:31:10.116 --> 00:31:11.276
Okay?


00:31:11.317 --> 00:31:13.114
Have a good night.


00:31:19.224 --> 00:31:20.213
Christ.


00:31:20.258 --> 00:31:22.226
Can I get inside?
It's cold.


00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.228
Sorry. Come on, this is a car.


00:31:24.262 --> 00:31:25.991
It's not a community
cent... Jesus Christ.


00:31:26.031 --> 00:31:27.555
Hey. Come on.


00:31:27.599 --> 00:31:29.157
(chuckling)


00:31:33.471 --> 00:31:34.438
- Um...
- So what are


00:31:34.472 --> 00:31:35.666
- you guys looking for?
- Huh?


00:31:36.774 --> 00:31:38.241
Oh, fuck. Fuck.


00:31:38.275 --> 00:31:40.004
Are you spying on us?


00:31:40.044 --> 00:31:41.102
- No.
- Not at all.


00:31:41.145 --> 00:31:42.736
- Who's this?
- It's insulting.


00:31:42.779 --> 00:31:43.871
- No, we're...
- Seriously, we've been...


00:31:43.913 --> 00:31:44.902
Did you not trust us.


00:31:44.948 --> 00:31:46.142
This your girlfriend?


00:31:46.182 --> 00:31:47.274
No, she's not my girlfriend.


00:31:47.317 --> 00:31:49.808
Trust-- if you have to say it,
it means there is none.


00:31:49.853 --> 00:31:51.115
Use a condom.


00:31:51.154 --> 00:31:52.587
That's my answer to trust.


00:31:52.622 --> 00:31:54.681
Sure, I trust you--
use a condom.


00:31:54.724 --> 00:31:56.122
One minute you're nice to me,


00:31:56.158 --> 00:31:57.921
the next you're spying on me.


00:31:57.960 --> 00:31:59.985
No, Erika. Erika, listen.


00:32:01.964 --> 00:32:03.397
Erika.


00:32:03.432 --> 00:32:04.524
Uh-oh.


00:32:04.566 --> 00:32:05.965
Please, please,
can you just listen? Please.


00:32:06.001 --> 00:32:10.994
50 pounds, <i>whatever</i> you want.


00:32:12.573 --> 00:32:13.870
What's your name?


00:32:13.908 --> 00:32:15.375
Except talk.


00:32:16.577 --> 00:32:17.874
What if I want to talk?


00:32:17.912 --> 00:32:19.311
Call the Samaritans.


00:32:19.347 --> 00:32:21.211
Oh, please.


00:32:21.248 --> 00:32:23.011
You know, I don't
have time for this.


00:32:23.049 --> 00:32:25.142
WOMAN:
Humans.


00:32:25.185 --> 00:32:26.209
Chat, chat, chat, chat, chat.


00:32:26.253 --> 00:32:27.880
We talk.


00:32:27.921 --> 00:32:29.013
Why?


00:32:29.055 --> 00:32:30.352
I don't know.


00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:33.086
Animals don't talk,
because they don't lie.


00:32:33.126 --> 00:32:34.217
Erika!


00:32:35.461 --> 00:32:37.224
A ridiculous idea.


00:32:40.132 --> 00:32:41.224
That was good?


00:32:41.267 --> 00:32:42.256
You really helped.


00:32:44.036 --> 00:32:45.435
We should, uh...


00:32:45.471 --> 00:32:47.461
We need,
we, we need to lock up.


00:32:47.505 --> 00:32:48.472
You work here?


00:32:48.506 --> 00:32:49.473
Yeah.


00:32:49.507 --> 00:32:50.474
Yes.


00:32:50.508 --> 00:32:51.805
Bad place for an office.


00:32:59.817 --> 00:33:01.340
What's your project about?


00:33:02.486 --> 00:33:04.044
I don't really know.


00:33:04.087 --> 00:33:07.523
A place I'd like to live in...
or run through.


00:33:07.558 --> 00:33:10.925
I would love
to jump, you know...


00:33:10.961 --> 00:33:12.860
Chik, chik, chik...
jump from...


00:33:12.895 --> 00:33:15.193
building to building.
Chika-chik, chik.


00:33:15.231 --> 00:33:16.858
(sighs)


00:33:16.899 --> 00:33:17.888
This is amazing.


00:33:17.934 --> 00:33:19.526
But what?


00:33:19.569 --> 00:33:22.902
No buts. Is that amazing?


00:33:22.939 --> 00:33:24.873
Yeah.


00:33:24.907 --> 00:33:26.203
But dangerous.


00:33:26.241 --> 00:33:27.401
Oh.


00:33:27.442 --> 00:33:28.409
(laughing)


00:33:28.443 --> 00:33:30.240
No buts.


00:33:30.278 --> 00:33:31.575
You see the city?


00:33:32.914 --> 00:33:34.108
What is it like?


00:33:36.585 --> 00:33:37.643
Better.


00:33:42.122 --> 00:33:45.182
Did my dad just
not want to come with us?


00:33:45.225 --> 00:33:50.390
Uh... He, um...


00:33:50.431 --> 00:33:52.898
He loved you,


00:33:52.932 --> 00:33:55.958
but, uh, he was engineer.


00:33:56.002 --> 00:33:58.903
He was needed.


00:33:58.938 --> 00:34:00.929
Every bridge was blown up. So...


00:34:02.275 --> 00:34:04.038
Anyway,


00:34:04.077 --> 00:34:05.270
it's more complicated.


00:34:07.279 --> 00:34:09.543
No story from Sarajevo
is simple.


00:34:12.551 --> 00:34:14.212
No! No!


00:34:16.555 --> 00:34:17.714
I don't want to.


00:34:17.755 --> 00:34:19.518
(indistinct shouting)


00:34:19.557 --> 00:34:21.081
No! No!


00:34:28.566 --> 00:34:30.556
(engine starts)


00:35:02.263 --> 00:35:04.163
WILL:
There's no bread.


00:35:04.198 --> 00:35:07.361
No bread, no butter,
no flour, no dairy.


00:35:08.736 --> 00:35:10.134
Nor last night.
You didn't notice.


00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:11.501
I did.


00:35:11.538 --> 00:35:13.438
Hmm.


00:35:13.473 --> 00:35:15.134
We're trying a diet.


00:35:16.376 --> 00:35:17.843
How's the chicken?


00:35:17.878 --> 00:35:19.038
Chicken's good.


00:35:19.079 --> 00:35:20.376
Good.


00:35:20.414 --> 00:35:22.142
Ice cream is dairy.


00:35:22.181 --> 00:35:24.513
Ice cream <i>is</i> dairy,


00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:25.517
That's right.


00:35:25.551 --> 00:35:26.609
Sweetie, try the chicken.


00:35:26.652 --> 00:35:27.846
WILL:
Try the chicken first.


00:35:27.887 --> 00:35:30.822
LIV:
This is a diet just for you.


00:35:30.856 --> 00:35:34.383
Bea, put those
things back, now.


00:35:34.427 --> 00:35:35.723
Have some chicken.


00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:37.523
Rosemary suggested
we try this.


00:35:37.562 --> 00:35:38.529
- Where is it?
- Remember her?


00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:39.892
- Where <i>is</i> it?
- Therapist Rosemary.


00:35:39.931 --> 00:35:41.728
What have you done
with the ice cream?


00:35:41.766 --> 00:35:42.733
There's amazing stuff


00:35:42.767 --> 00:35:45.531
about the affect
of food on mood,


00:35:45.603 --> 00:35:46.865
on the brain.


00:35:46.905 --> 00:35:47.871
Rosemary.


00:35:47.905 --> 00:35:48.872
I chose it!


00:35:48.906 --> 00:35:50.874
She said that?


00:35:50.908 --> 00:35:51.966
Wow.


00:35:52.009 --> 00:35:52.976
It's not fair!


00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:54.534
Every time I say
something in this house,


00:35:54.578 --> 00:35:55.909
it gets repeated
back as a question.


00:35:55.946 --> 00:35:57.072
You know I like ice cream!


00:35:57.114 --> 00:35:58.638
If I say "good morning,"
people ask me


00:35:58.682 --> 00:36:00.274
what makes me think
it's a good morning.


00:36:00.317 --> 00:36:01.544
- What?
- No ice cream!


00:36:01.617 --> 00:36:03.881
I'm trying to hold
this family together, Will.


00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:05.114
There's no ice cream, Mama.


00:36:05.154 --> 00:36:06.644
Who are "people"?
Who are these mysterious people?


00:36:06.689 --> 00:36:08.486
I feel a fool
with my sun box, I do,


00:36:08.591 --> 00:36:10.821
but it's the only sun
you can rely on in this house.


00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:12.919
- I want the ice cream!
- No, Liv... Hey, Liv, come on.


00:36:12.962 --> 00:36:14.588
- What are you talking about?
- Why have you taken it away?!


00:36:14.629 --> 00:36:15.596
I wanted a slice of bread.


00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:16.597
You know what? Really?


00:36:16.631 --> 00:36:17.928
Can we, can we just try this?


00:36:17.966 --> 00:36:19.456
Can we try this
as a family, please?


00:36:19.501 --> 00:36:20.900
Just as a family for a week?


00:36:20.936 --> 00:36:23.097
Don't say try together
if we don't decide together.


00:36:23.138 --> 00:36:24.332
Well, don't take
a side together,


00:36:24.372 --> 00:36:25.361
if we don't cook together


00:36:25.407 --> 00:36:26.873
or talk together
or do anything together.


00:36:26.907 --> 00:36:28.499
- It's not fair!
- We don't do anything together.


00:36:28.542 --> 00:36:29.634
- There we are.
- Exercise...


00:36:29.677 --> 00:36:30.701
- Exercise...
- "There we are" what?


00:36:30.745 --> 00:36:31.712
- Stop that.
- Exercise. Exercise...


00:36:31.746 --> 00:36:33.077
You don't even hear it, Will.


00:36:33.114 --> 00:36:34.843
- You don't even hear it.
- Exercise, exercise...


00:36:34.882 --> 00:36:35.849
Eat your chicken first!


00:36:35.883 --> 00:36:37.248
- No!
- Stop yelling.


00:36:37.284 --> 00:36:38.774
Chicken?
I'm a vegetarian.


00:36:38.819 --> 00:36:40.945
- Don't you yell at me.
- You don't eat vegetables.


00:36:40.987 --> 00:36:42.511
- It's disgusting!
- You don't eat vegetables.


00:36:42.622 --> 00:36:43.646
- Disgusting! Disgusting!
- Will you stop?


00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:46.818
(all shouting)


00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:49.727
No, it's disgusting!
No, leave me alone!


00:36:49.763 --> 00:36:50.855
(china breaking)


00:36:50.897 --> 00:36:53.524
Now, what we know is,
if we smash things


00:36:53.632 --> 00:36:55.623
and we scream and we shout,
we get our own way.


00:36:55.667 --> 00:36:58.192
That's what we know.
Or run away.


00:36:58.237 --> 00:37:01.798
Great... Great.


00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:03.137
That's great.


00:37:03.175 --> 00:37:06.143
(clattering plates, silverware)


00:37:06.177 --> 00:37:08.042
Liv.


00:37:11.783 --> 00:37:13.580
Liv, darling.


00:37:17.855 --> 00:37:19.981
(shouts):
Liv!


00:37:20.023 --> 00:37:21.581
Or we just say nothing!


00:37:21.625 --> 00:37:23.183
Because God forbid we ever...


00:37:23.226 --> 00:37:25.091
we ever say
what we actually meant!


00:37:25.128 --> 00:37:27.119
No, just let
the shutter come down!


00:37:30.867 --> 00:37:33.266
Anyway...


00:37:33.302 --> 00:37:35.497
I really have
to get back to the office.


00:38:13.272 --> 00:38:16.639
Bea, my car keys.
Where's the battery? Bea.


00:38:16.676 --> 00:38:18.610
Hey, I need the battery.


00:38:18.644 --> 00:38:20.339
We're human.
Don't punish us.


00:38:20.379 --> 00:38:21.676
We get fed up.


00:38:21.714 --> 00:38:25.171
Fed up.
Is that about the diet?


00:38:25.217 --> 00:38:26.707
No. It means upset.
I don't know why.


00:38:26.751 --> 00:38:28.241
It's a metaphor, remember?
We spoke about those.


00:38:28.286 --> 00:38:30.220
"Cried your eyes out" doesn't
mean your eyeballs fall out.


00:38:30.255 --> 00:38:31.483
Metaphors. "Fed up."


00:38:31.556 --> 00:38:32.784
It's nothing to do with food.


00:38:32.824 --> 00:38:33.882
Where's my battery, Bea?


00:38:33.925 --> 00:38:35.619
- It's in my box.
- Where's your box?


00:38:35.659 --> 00:38:37.217
It's my box.
You can't go in my box.


00:38:37.261 --> 00:38:38.626
- Well, you go get the battery.
- Bea.


00:38:38.662 --> 00:38:40.425
It's all right.
We're working it out.


00:38:40.464 --> 00:38:42.364
Bea, go and get
Will's battery.


00:38:48.471 --> 00:38:50.496
"Will's battery"?


00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:51.939
Can you explain to me


00:38:51.975 --> 00:38:53.806
why you suddenly need
to go to the office?


00:38:53.843 --> 00:38:55.242
- You know why.
- You're not the police.


00:38:55.278 --> 00:38:56.870
No, and the police
are not night watchmen.


00:38:56.913 --> 00:38:58.346
Nor are you.
Most wives would worry


00:38:58.381 --> 00:39:00.246
if their husbands went
cruising in King's Cross


00:39:00.283 --> 00:39:01.943
- every night.
- Come on.


00:39:01.984 --> 00:39:03.576
Hire a security company, Will.


00:39:03.619 --> 00:39:05.143
Most wives are married


00:39:05.187 --> 00:39:07.178
to their husbands, as we're
being so accurate tonight.


00:39:07.222 --> 00:39:10.157
Dads, wives, Will's battery.
Thanks for that.


00:39:10.192 --> 00:39:12.888
I don't see the real father
around much.


00:39:12.928 --> 00:39:15.157
I love your sun box,
whatever the fuck


00:39:15.196 --> 00:39:16.458
it's supposed to do.


00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:18.020
I keep hoping
it'll warm you up.


00:39:42.354 --> 00:39:44.151
Skinny cup, extra shot, right?


00:39:44.189 --> 00:39:45.679
Right.


00:39:48.327 --> 00:39:49.851
Thanks.


00:39:53.765 --> 00:39:56.131
Oh. I brought a CD.


00:39:56.167 --> 00:39:57.634
I've got some CDs.


00:39:57.669 --> 00:39:59.102
No. For dancing.


00:39:59.137 --> 00:40:00.627
I like to dance.


00:40:00.672 --> 00:40:02.503
In cars?


00:40:02.607 --> 00:40:04.973
Inside, outside, upside down.


00:40:05.009 --> 00:40:07.272
I can dance on your lap,
but it's not


00:40:07.311 --> 00:40:09.142
- for free.
- Absolutely.


00:40:09.179 --> 00:40:11.943
I still make you come,
but not inside me.


00:40:11.982 --> 00:40:15.213
Some guys think
that's not cheating.


00:40:15.252 --> 00:40:16.719
Hmm?


00:40:23.893 --> 00:40:29.763
# I need a man #


00:40:29.799 --> 00:40:32.926
# To make me love #


00:40:32.968 --> 00:40:36.904
# To make me sing #


00:40:36.938 --> 00:40:43.639
# I sing, I need a man #


00:40:43.678 --> 00:40:46.942
# To make me feel #


00:40:46.981 --> 00:40:50.542
# Like I'm a queen. #


00:40:50.584 --> 00:40:51.744
- (shuts off music)
- That's good.


00:40:51.785 --> 00:40:53.116
You are. You're very good.


00:40:53.153 --> 00:40:54.643
You're great.


00:41:01.761 --> 00:41:04.855
That's okay. It's fine.


00:41:04.897 --> 00:41:05.693
What do you mean?


00:41:05.732 --> 00:41:07.222
It's okay.


00:41:22.414 --> 00:41:25.211
(fox screeching)


00:41:27.718 --> 00:41:29.208
Hi.


00:41:34.558 --> 00:41:36.048
I'm sorry.


00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:38.884
You smell of perfume.


00:41:40.964 --> 00:41:43.626
I don't know how I do.


00:41:43.666 --> 00:41:45.224
Nor do I.


00:41:46.569 --> 00:41:49.264
I love you.


00:41:49.304 --> 00:41:51.670
Is that an answer?


00:41:51.707 --> 00:41:54.676
It's the truth.


00:41:54.710 --> 00:41:56.075
What do you need?


00:41:56.111 --> 00:41:57.544
What do you want ever?


00:41:57.646 --> 00:42:00.376
I feel as if I'm
tapping on a window.


00:42:00.416 --> 00:42:02.610
You're somewhere
behind the glass,


00:42:02.650 --> 00:42:03.947
but you can't hear me.


00:42:03.985 --> 00:42:05.680
Even when you're
angry, like now,


00:42:05.720 --> 00:42:08.484
it's like someone a long, long
way away is angry with me.


00:42:08.523 --> 00:42:12.892
Well, glass is better than ice,
which is where we were earlier.


00:42:12.927 --> 00:42:17.260
Sweden, ice, depression,
the high rate of suicide?


00:42:17.297 --> 00:42:18.730
Mm-hmm.


00:42:18.765 --> 00:42:21.962
I never got close to anybody who
didn't want to talk about that.


00:42:22.002 --> 00:42:24.630
- Mm.
- Or free love.


00:42:24.671 --> 00:42:26.298
- Liv Ullmann.
- Liv Ullmann.


00:42:26.339 --> 00:42:28.636
Who's Norwegian, by the way.


00:42:30.109 --> 00:42:32.634
Well, there are no other
Swedes to talk about?


00:42:32.678 --> 00:42:34.043
Nobody lives there.


00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:36.378
You could drive for hours
before you pass another Volvo.


00:42:36.415 --> 00:42:38.508
Abba.


00:42:38.584 --> 00:42:41.347
Let's face it.
It's a sad old list.


00:42:41.386 --> 00:42:42.853
What about the English?


00:42:42.887 --> 00:42:44.514
You brought us what? Sarcasm?


00:42:44.589 --> 00:42:46.318
- Mm.
- And the Beatles.


00:42:46.357 --> 00:42:47.915
# Yeah, yeah, yeah. #


00:42:47.959 --> 00:42:49.688
# Knowing me, knowing you. #
A-ha.


00:42:49.727 --> 00:42:50.955
